"""MINT-style benign multi-agent debate with Azure-hosted chat models.

This runner follows Becker et al.'s MINT protocol more closely than the
previous role-based debate:

* three *neutral* analytical agents share the same instructions;
* five sequential turns are used, rather than five rounds per agent;
* only one, position-balanced agent receives additional context;
* WinoGrande-style tasks require a forced answer choice (no abstention);
* clean, relevant-misinformation, irrelevant-misinformation, and
  irrelevant-true-information conditions can be compared; and
* logs report answer persistence and whether the context-exposed agent
  recovered from an initially wrong answer.

The system measures task accuracy and susceptibility/recovery under context;
it does not claim to directly measure a model's internal understanding.

NOTE on relevant-misinformation injection: the context handed to the
context-exposed agent is ALWAYS the dataset's literal "other" entry inside
misinformation_by_strategy, taken verbatim -- never a different strategy
variant, and never text generated by this script or the model. This is
hardcoded (not driven by a mutable config constant) so there's no risk of
silent drift, and the exact injected text is printed at runtime so you can
visually confirm it against the source JSON.
"""

